[
  {
    "contentHash": "2cd15d0e2ac68a35b4e2ec042d2cbd60d66f00752b3951b04111276fa41bb265",
    "heading": "Cell biology › Organelles",
    "text": "Mitochondria are the site of aerobic respiration. They make most of the cell's ATP, which is why muscle cells, which use a lot of energy, have many of them.

The nucleus holds the cell's DNA and controls which proteins the cell makes. It is surrounded by a double membrane called the nuclear envelope.

Ribosomes build proteins.",
    "words": 56,
  },
  {
    "contentHash": "053a7311c7911c91855eafa0cce4332ac55d141697e1e6c5eec81e60cb412280",
    "heading": "Cell biology › Membranes",
    "text": "- The cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer.
- It is selectively permeable: small non-polar molecules cross it freely, while ions need channel proteins.
- Cholesterol keeps it fluid at low temperatures.",
    "words": 32,
  },
]
`;

